To force a textfield to display columns in Adobe JavaScript, you can use the `columns` property of the textfield object. Here's a simple example of how you can set the number of columns for a textfield:

```javascript
// Assuming you have a textfield named 'myTextField'
myTextField.columns = 20; // Set the number of columns to 20
```

This will adjust the width of the textfield to accommodate the specified number of columns. If you need to set the number of columns dynamically, you can use a variable to store the desired number of columns and then update the `columns` property accordingly.

```javascript
// Example of setting the number of columns dynamically
var numberOfColumns = 20;
myTextField.columns = numberOfColumns;
```

Make sure that the textfield is properly initialized and that the `columns` property is supported by the textfield object in the context where you are using it.

